3
00:00:05,970 --> 00:00:08,950
on tubular interstitial
disease of the kidney.

4
00:00:11,990 --> 00:00:15,810
Today, we have several
major objectives. We will

5
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:19,250
describe the pathogenesis
of acutubular injury and

6
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:22,590
tubular interstitial
nephritis, including various

7
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:27,150
forms of peronephritis and
tubular interstitial nephritis

8
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:32,190
induced by drugs as well
as urate nephropathy.

9
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:39,370
I have included this table
in today's lecture as I

10
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:43,150
wanted to provide you with a
clear definition of acutubular

11
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:47,070
necrosis which is also
known as acutubular injury.

12
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:51,590
Acute tubular injury
represents one of the most

13
00:00:51,590 --> 00:00:54,490
important causes of
intrinsic renal failure

14
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:58,310
and along with pre-renal
and post-renal azotemia

15
00:00:58,310 --> 00:01:01,870
fall in the spectrum
of acute kidney injury.

16
00:01:02,130 --> 00:01:05,070
So today we will
focus only on acute

17
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:07,650
tubular injury while
the rest of conditions

18
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:09,950
will be presented
at a later stage.

19
00:01:12,830 --> 00:01:16,230
So let's describe
the acute tubular

20
00:01:16,230 --> 00:01:18,970
injury or acute
tubular necrosis.

21
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:21,930
This is a clinical
pathological entity

22
00:01:21,930 --> 00:01:25,350
characterized morphologically
by tubular injury,

23
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:29,010
more precisely by necrosis
of tubular epithelial

24
00:01:29,010 --> 00:01:32,510
cells, and clinically
by acute renal failure.

25
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:38,090
However, in some cases
we don't see necrosis. In

26
00:01:38,090 --> 00:01:41,490
this context, the term
acutubular injury seems

27
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:44,660
to be more appropriate
than acutubular necrosis.

28
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:49,490
So, acutubular injury is
determined by ischemia

29
00:01:49,490 --> 00:01:52,870
or by direct toxic
injury to the tubules.

30
00:01:53,690 --> 00:01:54,890
Ischemia.

31
00:01:55,350 --> 00:01:58,510
This is seen if the
blood flow is decreased

32
00:01:58,510 --> 00:02:02,810
or interrupted in
intrarenal blood vessels for

33
00:02:02,810 --> 00:02:05,830
example in the setting of
microscopic polyangiitis

34
00:02:05,830 --> 00:02:10,770
or microangiopathies
as seen in hemolytic

35
00:02:10,770 --> 00:02:15,050
uranic syndrome in
addition ischemia is

36
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:17,930
described in thrombotic
thrombocytopenic purpura

37
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:21,070
and it might be caused
by a reduced blood

38
00:02:21,070 --> 00:02:24,750
flow as describing
hypovolemic shock

39
00:02:26,050 --> 00:02:29,570
Regarding the direct toxic
injury to the tubules,

40
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:33,110
this might be induced
by endogenous agents

41
00:02:33,110 --> 00:02:35,930
such as myoglobin, hemoglobin,

42
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:39,670
monoclonal light chains,
bile, or bilirubin.

43
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:42,670
It may also be produced by

44
00:02:42,670 --> 00:02:45,210
exogenous agents such as drugs,

45
00:02:45,870 --> 00:02:47,650
radiocontrast dyes,

46
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:51,090
heavy metals, and
organic solvents.

47
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:57,070
Pathogenesis of
acutubular injury.

48
00:02:57,350 --> 00:03:00,770
The critical event in
acutubular injury is

49
00:03:00,770 --> 00:03:04,790
represented by tubular
injury caused by severe

50
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:07,350
and persistent
disturbances in blood flow.

51
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:10,970
As you might see
represented in this drawing,

52
00:03:11,610 --> 00:03:14,450
renal blood vessels
ischemia induces

53
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:17,490
initially two events,
tubular injury and

54
00:03:17,490 --> 00:03:20,110
a decreased glomerular
filtration rate.

55
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:24,150
Tubular injury may also
be caused by an agent

56
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:26,770
which is toxic for the
tubular epithelium.

57
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:31,390
Anyway, let's discuss
the ischemic pathway.

58
00:03:32,490 --> 00:03:35,830
Ischemia promotes severe
structural and functional

59
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:38,790
alterations in the
tubular epithelial cell.

60
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:43,350
This results in loss
of polarity of these

61
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:46,910
tubular cells as the
membrane proteins such

62
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:51,810
as the sodium potassium
ATPase is relocated

63
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:54,750
or redistributed from
its normal location.

64
00:03:55,630 --> 00:03:58,830
Subsequently, this
redistribution of sodium

65
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:02,610
potassium ATPase
determines an abnormal ion

66
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:06,850
transport across the
cells with large amounts

67
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:09,550
of sodium supplied to
the distal tubules.

68
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:11,990
This is not seen normally.

69
00:04:12,590 --> 00:04:16,370
This exaggerated
amount of sodium would

70
00:04:16,370 --> 00:04:19,370
then induce
vasoconstriction associated

71
00:04:19,370 --> 00:04:23,730
with a reduced GFR
as mentioned before.

72
00:04:24,310 --> 00:04:27,970
The ischemic or the toxic
injured tubular cells

73
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:31,530
release cytokines and
numerous leukocytes are

74
00:04:31,530 --> 00:04:35,810
attracted to promote an
interstitial inflammation.

75
00:04:37,090 --> 00:04:41,010
This is associated with
additional damage as

76
00:04:41,010 --> 00:04:44,030
injured cells separate
from the basement

77
00:04:44,030 --> 00:04:48,720
membrane and cause
luminal obstruction, thus

78
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:52,670
increasing the intratubular
pressure and decreasing

79
00:04:52,670 --> 00:04:55,670
further the glomerular
filtration rate.

80
00:04:56,910 --> 00:05:00,510
Tubular obstruction is
associated with a decreased

81
00:05:00,510 --> 00:05:05,530
urinary output, which may
also be induced in parallel

82
00:05:05,530 --> 00:05:08,470
by a tubular back leak
in the interstitium,

83
00:05:08,470 --> 00:05:13,030
causing interstitial edema
and further tubular damage.

84
00:05:16,810 --> 00:05:19,670
Regarding the
morphology of acutubular

85
00:05:19,670 --> 00:05:21,770
injury we distinguish
an ischemic as

86
00:05:21,770 --> 00:05:24,110
well as a toxic
morphological pattern.

87
00:05:24,350 --> 00:05:27,330
In the ischemic
acutubular injury

88
00:05:27,330 --> 00:05:30,810
pattern tubular
necrosis is patchy as

89
00:05:30,810 --> 00:05:33,310
short segments of
tubules are involved.

90
00:05:33,710 --> 00:05:37,470
These areas of necrosis are
are seen in the proximal

91
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:41,090
straight tubule, ascending
limbs of Henley's loop,

92
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:44,570
and sometime in the
proximal convoluted tubule.

93
00:05:44,850 --> 00:05:49,270
In the toxic acutubular
injury there is massive

94
00:05:49,270 --> 00:05:53,830
necrosis. This is seen in
the proximal straight tubule,

95
00:05:53,870 --> 00:05:57,330
proximal convoluted
tubule, and in the distal

96
00:05:57,330 --> 00:06:00,630
tubule, especially in the
ascending Henley's loop.

97
00:06:01,010 --> 00:06:04,770
In both types of
acutubular injury the

98
00:06:04,770 --> 00:06:07,890
lumens of the distal
converted tubules

99
00:06:07,890 --> 00:06:10,570
and collective
ducts contain casts.

100
00:06:11,170 --> 00:06:12,790
In conclusion,

101
00:06:12,930 --> 00:06:17,210
acutubular injury
morphology is represented by

102
00:06:17,570 --> 00:06:20,610
tubular epithelial
damage, more specific

103
00:06:20,610 --> 00:06:23,950
necrosis at multiple
points along the nephron.

104
00:06:24,610 --> 00:06:27,090
Between the damaged tubular

105
00:06:27,090 --> 00:06:29,590
segments there are
large skip areas.

106
00:06:30,110 --> 00:06:33,710
All of these may be associated
with rupture of basement

107
00:06:33,710 --> 00:06:37,790
membranes and occlusion of
tubular lumens by casts.

108
00:06:39,830 --> 00:06:43,830
Well in general histological
evaluation in these

109
00:06:43,830 --> 00:06:46,770
cases will show as you
can see on the left side

110
00:06:46,770 --> 00:06:50,830
histological image necrosis
with detachment of the

111
00:06:50,830 --> 00:06:54,610
epithelial tubular cells
in the proximal convoluted

112
00:06:54,610 --> 00:06:57,930
tubule as well as
proximal straight tubule.

113
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:01,050
Gromerular as well
as distal convoluted

114
00:07:01,050 --> 00:07:04,390
tubule structures
are not affected.

115
00:07:04,940 --> 00:07:07,950
On the right side image
you see some necrotic

116
00:07:07,950 --> 00:07:11,170
cells which have separated
from their basement

117
00:07:11,170 --> 00:07:14,270
membrane and now are
filling the tubular lumens.

118
00:07:15,030 --> 00:07:17,130
Subsequently at
this level casts

119
00:07:17,130 --> 00:07:18,990
and obstruction will develop.

120
00:07:25,010 --> 00:07:27,030
Now I would like to
present some specific

121
00:07:27,030 --> 00:07:31,550
examples of acutubular
injury histology. In

122
00:07:31,550 --> 00:07:35,070
this first image you may
distinguish some dilated

123
00:07:35,070 --> 00:07:37,670
tubules lined by an
atrophic epithelium.

124
00:07:38,030 --> 00:07:41,550
Some of these tubules display a

125
00:07:41,550 --> 00:07:44,290
brownish pink
accellular pigment cast.

126
00:07:44,430 --> 00:07:48,230
This type of casts are
formed by the position

127
00:07:48,230 --> 00:07:51,010
of a non-protein
pigment that is

128
00:07:51,010 --> 00:07:54,930
released from either
myoglobin or hemoglobin.

129
00:07:56,850 --> 00:08:00,850
On this slide the left
the microscopic image

130
00:08:00,850 --> 00:08:03,770
represents a tissue section
of kidney stained with

131
00:08:03,770 --> 00:08:07,390
the usual hematoxylineosin.
We see that tubular

132
00:08:07,390 --> 00:08:09,850
epithelial cells
significantly enlarge and we

133
00:08:09,850 --> 00:08:12,950
say that they are
ballooning. This type of

134
00:08:12,950 --> 00:08:17,270
pattern of acutubular injury
is produced by ingestion

135
00:08:17,270 --> 00:08:20,130
of ethylene glycol
which is a toxic agent.

136
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:24,370
As you may note on the right
side microphotograph, if

137
00:08:24,370 --> 00:08:27,490
you examine these cases
under polarizing microscope,

138
00:08:27,670 --> 00:08:31,250
we may identify calcium oxalate

139
00:08:31,250 --> 00:08:33,110
crystals in the tubular lumen.

140
00:08:34,030 --> 00:08:36,390
These crystals
will subsequently

141
00:08:36,390 --> 00:08:38,630
promote tubular obstruction.

142
00:08:41,770 --> 00:08:45,570
As mentioned earlier,
today I would also like to

143
00:08:45,570 --> 00:08:48,830
define the concept of tubular
interstitial nephritis.

144
00:08:48,970 --> 00:08:53,410
In brief, it is an entity
which includes numerous

145
00:08:53,410 --> 00:08:56,410
pathological conditions
characterized by

146
00:08:56,410 --> 00:08:59,070
inflammation of the
tubules and interstitial.

147
00:08:59,450 --> 00:09:02,550
Tubular interstitial
nephritis is clinically

148
00:09:02,550 --> 00:09:06,830
distinguished from glomerular
disease as the patients do

149
00:09:06,830 --> 00:09:09,750
not present with nephritic
or nephrotic syndrome.

150
00:09:10,190 --> 00:09:13,850
These patients instead
would show impaired ability

151
00:09:13,850 --> 00:09:16,950
to concentrate urine which
translates clinically

152
00:09:16,950 --> 00:09:20,230
in polyuria or nocturia
and salt wasting.

153
00:09:20,790 --> 00:09:24,110
They will also develop
metabolic acidosis and a

154
00:09:24,110 --> 00:09:27,370
variety of abnormalities
in tubular reabsorption or

155
00:09:27,370 --> 00:09:31,910
secretion that are specific
for various clinical forms.

156
00:09:32,250 --> 00:09:35,030
Tubular interstitial
nephritis might present

157
00:09:35,030 --> 00:09:38,930
as an acute or
chronic disease. The

158
00:09:38,930 --> 00:09:42,330
acute form has a rapid
onset and histologically

159
00:09:42,330 --> 00:09:44,850
is associated with
interstitial edema,

160
00:09:45,110 --> 00:09:47,910
loycocytic infiltration
in both tubules and

161
00:09:47,910 --> 00:09:50,810
interstitium, and of
course tubular injury.

162
00:09:51,250 --> 00:09:55,210
In chronic forms of
tubular interstitial

163
00:09:55,210 --> 00:09:57,790
nephritis, the kidney
shows a chronic

164
00:09:57,790 --> 00:10:00,490
inflammation with
mononuclear cells,

165
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:03,510
fibrosis, and tubular atrophy.

166
00:10:06,070 --> 00:10:09,070
From an etiopathogenic
perspective,

167
00:10:09,070 --> 00:10:11,250
we classify the
tubular interstitial

168
00:10:11,250 --> 00:10:14,070
nephritis in primary
and secondary.

169
00:10:14,290 --> 00:10:16,710
The most important primary

170
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:18,850
forms are caused by infections.

171
00:10:19,630 --> 00:10:23,390
These are the acute and
chronic pyelonephritis.

172
00:10:23,870 --> 00:10:28,430
However, viruses and
parasites might determine also

173
00:10:28,430 --> 00:10:32,450
infections forms of tubular
interstitial nephritis.

174
00:10:34,450 --> 00:10:39,170
Then there are numerous
drugs such as sulfonamides,

175
00:10:39,210 --> 00:10:40,390
meticillin,

176
00:10:40,670 --> 00:10:41,850
ampicillin,

177
00:10:41,970 --> 00:10:43,050
rifampin,

178
00:10:43,530 --> 00:10:47,570
diuretics and cymetidine,
as well as analgesics

179
00:10:47,570 --> 00:10:51,730
and heavy metals, lead
and cadmium, which may

180
00:10:51,730 --> 00:10:55,230
induce a primary tubular
interstitial nephritis.

181
00:10:56,150 --> 00:11:00,870
Then metabolic conditions
such as urate nephropathy,

182
00:11:00,870 --> 00:11:05,270
nephrocalcinosis, and
oxalate nephropathy may

183
00:11:05,270 --> 00:11:08,470
be associated with tubular
interstitial nephritis.

184
00:11:09,130 --> 00:11:13,710
Tubo-interstitial
nephritis is also described

185
00:11:13,710 --> 00:11:16,240
in chronic urinary
tract obstructions,

186
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:19,270
various cancers and
in multiple myeloma.

187
00:11:19,730 --> 00:11:23,850
It is also seen in transplant
rejection patients,

188
00:11:23,850 --> 00:11:28,260
in those with Sjogren's
syndrome and sarcoidosis.

189
00:11:29,690 --> 00:11:33,090
Also, a primary form
is seen in various

190
00:11:33,090 --> 00:11:37,190
vascular diseases, or in
nephronophthesis, which

191
00:11:37,190 --> 00:11:40,190
is also known as
medullary cystic disease.

192
00:11:41,650 --> 00:11:44,750
In some cases of primary

193
00:11:44,750 --> 00:11:46,630
tubular interstitial nephritis,

194
00:11:46,830 --> 00:11:50,190
no etiological factor
may be identified,

195
00:11:50,350 --> 00:11:53,410
and these cases are idiopathic.

196
00:11:54,290 --> 00:11:57,350
In addition, a secondary
form of tubular

197
00:11:57,350 --> 00:12:00,870
interstitial nephritis
has been reported

198
00:12:00,870 --> 00:12:03,810
in polycystic kidney
disease and diabetes

199
00:12:06,350 --> 00:12:09,510
Well, I must say that
it's beyond the scope of

200
00:12:09,510 --> 00:12:12,470
this presentation to
discuss all these conditions

201
00:12:12,470 --> 00:12:16,790
in detail Today, we
will focus on infectious

202
00:12:16,790 --> 00:12:19,950
and drug-induced tubular
interstitial nephritis

203
00:12:19,950 --> 00:12:23,810
and we will also describe
the ureid nephropathy.

204
00:12:26,730 --> 00:12:31,250
Penal nephritis, by
definition it represents an

205
00:12:31,250 --> 00:12:35,730
inflammatory process which
includes the tubules,

206
00:12:36,270 --> 00:12:39,530
the interstitium and
the renal pelvic.

207
00:12:41,220 --> 00:12:43,530
Regarding the
spread of infection

208
00:12:43,530 --> 00:12:46,450
and the associated
inflammation.

209
00:12:47,170 --> 00:12:51,030
We distinguish a
hematogenous and an ascending

210
00:12:51,030 --> 00:12:54,990
spread, and as indicated
in this drawing,

211
00:12:55,430 --> 00:12:59,990
a hematogenous spread is
seen in septicemic patients.

212
00:13:00,170 --> 00:13:02,530
Most of these cases are caused

213
00:13:02,530 --> 00:13:05,010
by Staphylococcus and E. coli.

214
00:13:06,330 --> 00:13:08,850
Hematogenous spread
might also be seen

215
00:13:08,850 --> 00:13:11,730
in patients with
infective endocarditis,

216
00:13:12,590 --> 00:13:15,770
ureteral obstruction
and immunosuppression.

217
00:13:16,230 --> 00:13:19,090
As illustrated in this drawing,

218
00:13:19,230 --> 00:13:22,330
an ascending infection
might be caused by E. coli,

219
00:13:22,630 --> 00:13:24,630
proteus and enterobacter.

220
00:13:25,390 --> 00:13:27,790
In these cases, there
is a combination

221
00:13:27,790 --> 00:13:30,050
of urinary bladder infection,

222
00:13:30,730 --> 00:13:34,770
vesico-ureteral reflux
and intrarenal reflux.

223
00:13:36,510 --> 00:13:40,950
However, more than 80%
of cases of pelonephritis

224
00:13:40,950 --> 00:13:44,830
are determined by
urinary tract infections

225
00:13:46,990 --> 00:13:50,070
Accurate pelonephritis
This is an acute

226
00:13:50,070 --> 00:13:53,550
superlative inflammation
of the kidney Determined in

227
00:13:53,550 --> 00:13:57,050
most cases by a bacterial
infection and rarely

228
00:13:57,050 --> 00:14:00,760
by a viral agent such
as polioma virus The

229
00:14:01,030 --> 00:14:04,710
patient has an ascending
urinary tract infections

230
00:14:04,710 --> 00:14:07,890
frequently This is
caused, as I mentioned,

231
00:14:08,090 --> 00:14:13,230
in most cases by E. coli
Or the patient might

232
00:14:13,230 --> 00:14:16,790
present with hematogenous
spread The laboratory

233
00:14:16,790 --> 00:14:21,250
investigation will show in
these cases White blood cells

234
00:14:21,250 --> 00:14:24,990
in the urine, as well as
white blood cell casts

235
00:14:25,230 --> 00:14:28,570
These patients
clinically have fever,

236
00:14:28,570 --> 00:14:31,150
pain in the costal
vertebral angle,

237
00:14:31,510 --> 00:14:33,950
nausea, vomiting, and chills.

238
00:14:35,780 --> 00:14:39,130
Apart from development of
chronic telonephritis, the

239
00:14:39,130 --> 00:14:43,570
most important complications
are papillary necrosis,

240
00:14:43,570 --> 00:14:47,650
pyonephrosis, and peripheral
abscess. Now I would

241
00:14:47,650 --> 00:14:52,190
like to define very briefly
each of these entities.

242
00:14:52,890 --> 00:14:57,290
Papillary necrosis is seen
in patients with urinary

243
00:14:57,290 --> 00:15:01,330
tract obstruction, diabetes,
and sickle cell disease.

244
00:15:01,810 --> 00:15:05,570
In these cases, one
or all the pyramids

245
00:15:05,570 --> 00:15:07,970
of the affected kidney
might be involved.

246
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:12,290
Pionephrosis develops
in a patient with total

247
00:15:12,290 --> 00:15:16,430
or almost total urinary
tract obstruction.

248
00:15:17,450 --> 00:15:21,030
The exudate accumulates and
as there is no drainage,

249
00:15:21,030 --> 00:15:25,130
it fills the renal pelvic,
calluses and ureter.

250
00:15:26,310 --> 00:15:29,630
Regarding the
perinephric abscess, this

251
00:15:29,630 --> 00:15:31,910
develops when the
superlative inflammation

252
00:15:31,910 --> 00:15:35,130
extends into the
perinephric tissue.

253
00:15:38,170 --> 00:15:40,590
Let's review now
some morphological

254
00:15:40,590 --> 00:15:43,270
forms of acute pylonephritis.

255
00:15:44,070 --> 00:15:47,010
In this image, there
is a dissected kidney

256
00:15:47,010 --> 00:15:50,310
removed during an
autopsy, which shows renal

257
00:15:50,310 --> 00:15:53,870
papillary necrosis extending
to several pyramids.

258
00:15:53,970 --> 00:15:57,690
The affected regions
are dark, but the tissue

259
00:15:57,690 --> 00:16:01,150
bordering renal papillary
necrosis is hyperemic.

260
00:16:06,350 --> 00:16:09,810
This microscopic image
displays the external

261
00:16:09,810 --> 00:16:13,410
surface of a dissected
kidney. The surface

262
00:16:13,410 --> 00:16:17,550
of this kidney shows
numerous grayish white

263
00:16:17,550 --> 00:16:21,190
areas suggestive of
perinephric abscesses

264
00:16:23,300 --> 00:16:26,770
On this slide we have
two micro photographs of

265
00:16:26,770 --> 00:16:29,610
kidney sections taken
from patients with acrid

266
00:16:29,610 --> 00:16:34,150
paleonephritis in both which
see a neutrophilic infiltrate

267
00:16:34,150 --> 00:16:38,390
in tubules and interstitium
Anyway, I would like

268
00:16:38,390 --> 00:16:42,290
to highlight that
histological evaluation has no

269
00:16:42,290 --> 00:16:46,230
role in the management
of these patients and is

270
00:16:46,230 --> 00:16:50,490
not routinely performed The
diagnosis is established

271
00:16:50,490 --> 00:16:53,570
on clinical, radiological
and medical laboratory

272
00:16:53,570 --> 00:16:57,570
grounds, including renal
CT and urine evaluation

273
00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:06,360
Chronic pyelonephritis
Compared with acute

274
00:17:06,360 --> 00:17:09,940
pyelonephritis, this entity
represents a chronic inflammation

275
00:17:09,940 --> 00:17:13,340
and scarring involving
the tubules, the

276
00:17:13,340 --> 00:17:17,120
interstitium, as well as
the calluses and the pelvic.

277
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:21,020
The patients usually
had the previous acute

278
00:17:21,020 --> 00:17:25,520
pyelonephritis and
vesicovatorial reflux, or

279
00:17:25,520 --> 00:17:30,200
they had in the past
obstructing renal stones.

280
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:33,280
These patients
have numerous prior

281
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:36,600
recurrent episodes of
acute pyelonephritis

282
00:17:36,600 --> 00:17:39,840
or have been
inappropriately treated.

283
00:17:40,850 --> 00:17:43,100
Also, I would like
to add that chronic

284
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:47,140
pyelonephritis is seen
in 10-20% of renal

285
00:17:47,140 --> 00:17:50,100
transplants or in
dialysis patients.

286
00:17:52,160 --> 00:17:57,020
Clinically, these individuals
would present with a

287
00:17:57,020 --> 00:18:00,400
gradual onset of renal
insufficiency and hypertension.

288
00:18:00,720 --> 00:18:03,880
Later, they will
develop proteinuria

289
00:18:03,880 --> 00:18:06,420
and end-stage renal failure.

290
00:18:09,460 --> 00:18:13,260
Chronic pyelonephritis
morphology As you may

291
00:18:13,260 --> 00:18:17,560
see here in this
drawing and microscopic

292
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:21,100
image, the kidneys with
chronic pyelonephritis

293
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:24,640
are asymmetric and
irregularly scarred.

294
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:28,120
Usually asymmetry is
identified radiologically.

295
00:18:28,750 --> 00:18:32,640
On the right side image
there is a dissected kidney

296
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:34,900
and we can distinguish a u

297
00:18:34,900 --> 00:18:36,980
-shaped corticomedullary scar

298
00:18:37,050 --> 00:18:40,880
which overlies a
blunted deformed calyx.

299
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:44,480
All of these are
characteristic for

300
00:18:44,480 --> 00:18:48,020
chronic pyelonephritis
histology.

301
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:54,360
In chronic
pyelonephritis cases,

302
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:57,160
histological evaluation,
as you may observe

303
00:18:57,160 --> 00:19:00,520
on this microscopic
image, reveals a massive

304
00:19:00,520 --> 00:19:03,720
chronic infiltrate
made of lymphocytes and

305
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:06,700
monocytes in the tubules
and interstitium.

306
00:19:09,620 --> 00:19:15,140
In addition, as represented
in these images. In some

307
00:19:15,140 --> 00:19:19,100
cases there is significant
formation of eosinophilic

308
00:19:19,100 --> 00:19:23,300
casts in the tubules. This
histological pattern is

309
00:19:23,300 --> 00:19:27,120
similar in a way with normal
structure of the thyroid.

310
00:19:27,240 --> 00:19:30,820
Therefore we call this pattern

311
00:19:30,820 --> 00:19:32,980
thyroidization of kidney.

312
00:19:33,420 --> 00:19:37,800
This is typical for
chronic paranefritis and is

313
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:40,660
associated with broadcasts
in the urine sediment.

314
00:19:43,020 --> 00:19:46,300
Let's discuss now the
primary tubular interstitial

315
00:19:46,300 --> 00:19:50,300
nephritis induced by drugs.
As I mentioned drugs and

316
00:19:50,300 --> 00:19:53,880
toxic agents are the second
most common cause of tubular

317
00:19:53,880 --> 00:19:57,800
interstitial nephritis
after pilonephritis. This

318
00:19:57,800 --> 00:20:00,760
condition is triggered by
an interstitial immunologic

319
00:20:00,760 --> 00:20:04,640
reaction in which small
molecules of drugs called

320
00:20:04,640 --> 00:20:09,140
haptenes bind to tubular
epithelium. This triggers an

321
00:20:09,140 --> 00:20:12,940
IgE immunological reaction
against haptin-bound

322
00:20:12,940 --> 00:20:17,520
tubular cells and their
basement membrane. The outcome

323
00:20:17,520 --> 00:20:21,420
is tubular injury and an
acute inflammatory reaction.

324
00:20:22,440 --> 00:20:23,340
Clinically,

325
00:20:23,580 --> 00:20:28,600
the patient present at
15 days post-exposure

326
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:32,420
to a pharmacological
agent with fever,

327
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:33,700
eosinophilia,

328
00:20:34,180 --> 00:20:38,940
skin rash and oliguria
in about 50% of cases

329
00:20:38,990 --> 00:20:42,060
As mentioned before,
the most important

330
00:20:42,060 --> 00:20:45,580
drugs leading to this type
of tubular interstitial

331
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:49,460
nephritis are
sulfonamides, metisilin,

332
00:20:49,680 --> 00:20:52,340
ampicillin rifampin,

333
00:20:52,680 --> 00:20:54,960
diuretics and cymetidine

334
00:20:57,550 --> 00:21:01,540
Based on this image
included here, I would like

335
00:21:01,540 --> 00:21:05,320
to describe the morphology
in the acute forms

336
00:21:05,320 --> 00:21:08,280
of drug-induced tubular
interstitial nephritis.

337
00:21:08,520 --> 00:21:12,460
So this microphotograph
is the hematoxylineosin

338
00:21:12,460 --> 00:21:17,260
stained kidney section, which
shows a variable interstitial

339
00:21:17,260 --> 00:21:20,240
edema and infiltration
with lymphocytes and

340
00:21:20,240 --> 00:21:24,600
macrophages, as well as
neutrophils and eosinophils.

341
00:21:24,880 --> 00:21:28,840
There is also tubulitis
suggested in this

342
00:21:28,840 --> 00:21:32,240
microscopic image by
polymorphonuclears

343
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:35,320
and lymphocytes
infiltrating the tubules.

344
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:39,980
These features are seen
better in the kidney medulla

345
00:21:39,980 --> 00:21:43,510
where most of the drugs
are initially concentrated.

346
00:21:48,650 --> 00:21:51,870
Here is another
microscopic image which

347
00:21:51,870 --> 00:21:54,370
shows an additional
example of drug-induced

348
00:21:54,370 --> 00:21:56,810
primary tubular
interstitial nephritis.

349
00:21:56,810 --> 00:22:00,390
In this case, there
is interstitial edema.

350
00:22:00,630 --> 00:22:03,290
Also, there are
numerous lymphocytes and

351
00:22:03,290 --> 00:22:06,590
macrophages in the
interstitium and tubules,

352
00:22:06,590 --> 00:22:09,230
as well as osinophils
in the interstitium.

353
00:22:14,410 --> 00:22:16,710
Today, I also like
to discuss the

354
00:22:16,710 --> 00:22:20,350
non-steroidal anti
-inflammatory drugs

355
00:22:21,030 --> 00:22:24,030
induced tubular
interstitial nephritis.

356
00:22:24,210 --> 00:22:28,470
Non-steroidal anti
-inflammatory drugs, NSADs, are

357
00:22:28,470 --> 00:22:31,830
very common and are
administered to a wide range of

358
00:22:31,830 --> 00:22:35,070
patients, including those
at risk for renal disease.

359
00:22:35,310 --> 00:22:39,890
Numerous NSAIDs are non
-selective cyclooxygenase

360
00:22:39,890 --> 00:22:43,270
inhibitors, and their
adverse renal effects

361
00:22:43,270 --> 00:22:46,310
are related to their
ability to inhibit

362
00:22:46,310 --> 00:22:50,030
cyclooxygenase-dependent
prostaglandin synthesis.

363
00:22:50,690 --> 00:22:54,340
On a special note, the
selective cyclooxygenase

364
00:22:54,730 --> 00:22:59,710
2 inhibitors do not
induce any lesion on the

365
00:22:59,710 --> 00:23:02,630
gastrointestinal tract, but
they do affect the kidneys

366
00:23:02,630 --> 00:23:06,670
as these organs are rich
in cyclooxygenase 2.

367
00:23:07,350 --> 00:23:11,530
If the dose of NSAIDs
is high, the patient

368
00:23:11,530 --> 00:23:15,930
may present not
with classic tubular

369
00:23:15,930 --> 00:23:18,290
interstitial nephritis,
but with an acute

370
00:23:18,290 --> 00:23:21,910
kidney injury, including
papillary necrosis.

371
00:23:22,180 --> 00:23:24,290
In these cases,

372
00:23:24,490 --> 00:23:29,250
the NSAIDs are toxic and
they decrease significantly

373
00:23:29,250 --> 00:23:32,510
the vasodilatory
prostaglandin synthesis,

374
00:23:32,510 --> 00:23:37,010
inducing ischemia.
Otherwise, if the dose is

375
00:23:37,010 --> 00:23:40,050
not toxic, they might
produce acute tubular

376
00:23:40,050 --> 00:23:44,850
interstitial nephritis
by hypersensitivity, IgE,

377
00:23:44,850 --> 00:23:49,330
immunological mechanism,
as described earlier.

378
00:23:49,950 --> 00:23:55,190
Some patients would present
after NSAIDs with an

379
00:23:55,190 --> 00:23:58,840
acute form of tubular
interstitial nephritis associated

380
00:23:59,130 --> 00:24:02,570
with minimal change disease,
which is characterized

381
00:24:02,570 --> 00:24:07,150
by renal failure and
nephrotic syndrome. In

382
00:24:07,150 --> 00:24:10,430
these cases, apart
from the immunological

383
00:24:10,430 --> 00:24:14,370
hypersensitivity reaction,
there is also a significant

384
00:24:14,370 --> 00:24:19,650
injury to podocytes, most
likely caused by cytokines,

385
00:24:19,650 --> 00:24:23,790
which are overexpressed
in an inflammatory state.

386
00:24:24,850 --> 00:24:29,530
Rarely, after NSAIDs, the
patients would develop

387
00:24:29,530 --> 00:24:33,170
a membranous nephropathy
with nephrotic syndrome,

388
00:24:33,170 --> 00:24:36,770
but pathogenesis in
these cases is not clear.

389
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:41,190
So we might conclude
that in addition to an

390
00:24:41,190 --> 00:24:45,850
immunological IgE
hypersensitivity related tubular

391
00:24:45,850 --> 00:24:50,010
interstitial nephritis,
the NSAIDs may induce

392
00:24:50,010 --> 00:24:53,970
an acute tubular
interstitial nephritis with

393
00:24:53,970 --> 00:24:57,850
minimal change disease
and nephrotic syndrome, or

394
00:24:57,850 --> 00:25:01,770
they might cause acute
kidney injury or membranous

395
00:25:01,770 --> 00:25:04,710
nephropathy also with
nephrotic syndrome.

396
00:25:07,870 --> 00:25:11,010
Now, in the end of today's
lecture, I would like

397
00:25:11,010 --> 00:25:13,550
to talk about a very
important metabolic

398
00:25:13,550 --> 00:25:16,890
condition, the urethed
nephropathy, which may be

399
00:25:16,890 --> 00:25:19,730
associated with tubular
interstitial nephritis.

400
00:25:20,970 --> 00:25:24,310
There is an acute and a
chronic form of urethed

401
00:25:24,310 --> 00:25:29,330
nephropathy. Let's discuss
first the acute form. The acute

402
00:25:29,330 --> 00:25:34,970
form, or more correctly, the
acute uric acid nephropathy,

403
00:25:34,970 --> 00:25:39,370
is seen in patients with
leukemias and lymphomas

404
00:25:39,370 --> 00:25:43,170
who develop tumor lysis
syndrome during chemotherapy.

405
00:25:43,970 --> 00:25:46,990
An increased level of uric acid

406
00:25:46,990 --> 00:25:50,530
results from
purine degradation.

407
00:25:50,730 --> 00:25:54,250
These are key components
of cell and are

408
00:25:54,250 --> 00:25:58,170
seen as monomeric
precursors of DNA and RNA.

409
00:25:58,650 --> 00:26:02,330
Anyway, chemotherapy destroys
a significant number of

410
00:26:02,330 --> 00:26:07,190
cells which release an
increased amount of uric acid.

411
00:26:08,610 --> 00:26:12,830
subsequently in the
collecting tubules an acidic

412
00:26:12,830 --> 00:26:17,050
pH promotes precipitation
of this uric acid

413
00:26:17,050 --> 00:26:23,130
and the final outcome is
the obstruction of nephrons

414
00:26:23,130 --> 00:26:27,750
and an acute renal
failure determined by this

415
00:26:27,750 --> 00:26:32,090
increased amounts of
precipitated uric acid.

416
00:26:33,070 --> 00:26:36,530
The chronic form of urate
nephropathy is associated

417
00:26:36,530 --> 00:26:39,870
with gout, hence the
name gouty nephropathy.

418
00:26:40,410 --> 00:26:43,530
Gout will be presented
in detail at a later

419
00:26:43,530 --> 00:26:47,130
stage, but now I would
like to mention that this

420
00:26:47,130 --> 00:26:50,030
condition is characterized
by the position of

421
00:26:50,030 --> 00:26:54,390
monosodium urate crystals
in the acidic environment

422
00:26:54,390 --> 00:26:58,930
of the distal tubules
and collecting ducts.

423
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:03,370
As a result of this
deposition they will form

424
00:27:03,370 --> 00:27:06,370
distinct birefringent
needle-like crystals.

425
00:27:06,760 --> 00:27:11,070
These are seen in the tubules
and in the interstitium.

426
00:27:11,200 --> 00:27:14,990
These crystals determine
a foreign body joint cell

427
00:27:14,990 --> 00:27:19,890
reaction represented by
accumulation of mononuclear cells

428
00:27:19,890 --> 00:27:25,870
around a crystal forming a
tofus which obstructs the

429
00:27:25,870 --> 00:27:30,030
tubules and determine
cortical atrophy and scarring.

430
00:27:31,710 --> 00:27:36,830
The microscopic image inserted
on this slide represents

431
00:27:36,830 --> 00:27:40,890
a kidney section in
which medulla displays a

432
00:27:40,890 --> 00:27:45,450
crystal urate tofus surrounded
by elongated macrophages

433
00:27:45,450 --> 00:27:48,610
suggesting a chronic
granulomatous inflammation.

434
00:27:48,910 --> 00:27:51,730
This is characteristic for a

435
00:27:51,730 --> 00:27:55,210
chronic urate or
gouty nephropathy.

436
00:27:55,630 --> 00:27:57,890
Thank you very much
for your attention.

437
00:27:58,280 --> 00:28:00,690
Please let me know if you
have any issues related

438
00:28:00,690 --> 00:28:04,230
to this material and good
luck with your studies.

439
00:28:04,370 --> 00:28:07,310
Once again, thank you
very much. Bye-bye.